Added `cancel` to `src/main/java/mn/pay/PaymentService.java:39`:

```java
@Transactional
public Payment cancel(String paymentId) {
    Payment p = repository.findById(paymentId)
            .orElseThrow(() -> new IllegalArgumentException("unknown payment: " + paymentId));
    if (!"PENDING".equals(p.getStatus())) {
        throw new IllegalStateException(
                "only a PENDING payment can be cancelled, was " + p.getStatus());
    }
    p.setStatus("CANCELLED");
    return repository.save(p);
}
```

Decisions worth flagging:

- **`@Transactional`** to match `create`, so the read of the current status and the write can't interleave with a concurrent cancel of the same payment. Note this gives you read-committed-level protection, not a guarantee — two concurrent cancels can still both pass the check under the default isolation. If double-cancel needs to be strictly impossible, that wants a `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` (`@Lock(PESSIMISTIC_WRITE)` on a repository finder) or a conditional `UPDATE ... WHERE status = 'PENDING'`. I didn't add either, since the existing code doesn't use them and it changes the repository contract.
- **Exceptions**: `IllegalArgumentException` for an unknown id, `IllegalStateException` for a non-PENDING one — consistent with `PaymentValidator`'s use of `IllegalArgumentException`. Cancelling an already-CANCELLED payment throws rather than being a no-op; say the word if you'd rather it be idempotent.
- **Status as a `String`** literal, mirroring `create`'s `"PENDING"`. An enum would be safer but would touch the entity and migration.

`mvn compile` and `mvn test` both pass. I kept to the service method as asked — no test for `cancel` (the existing `PaymentServiceTest` passes `null` for the repository, so covering this needs a mock), and no `cancelPayment` mutation in `PaymentController`/`payment.graphqls`. Happy to add either.
